OGRE patch: the RTSS's linear skinning blend reads the bone matrices where they are declared.

Origin: torchlight-rekindled (https://github.com/GreenSwampDweller/torchlight-rekindled),
patches/ogre/0024-rtss-skinning-blend-without-array-copy.patch at commit
42e9bac358b3f260baefd6efa380e0864895c2a7 (2026-10-20), GPL-3.0, by its authors. Taken unchanged
(it applies cleanly to OGRE v14.6.0; written for v14.5.2); this header is ours.

Why we take it: Mali's compiler copies the `in` bone palette of blendBonesMat3x4 to the stack for
every vertex (malioc, Mali-G615, our skinned programs: 3 KB of stack, ~470 load/store cycles a
vertex; ~35 once the blend indexes the uniform in place). The library file is shared by GLSL,
GLSL ES and HLSL (OgreUnifiedShader.h), so Direct3D 11 gets the macro too.

The original description follows.

Torchlight: RTSS's linear skinning blend reads the bone matrices where they are
declared instead of through a copy. `blendBonesMat3x4` took the whole
`mat3x4 bones_mat[BONE_COUNT]` uniform array as an `in` parameter, which GLSL
copies - up to 64 matrices a vertex on the phone - and Adreno 750's compiler
(GLES 3.2, the Android build) got that copy wrong: vertices of skinned models
were thrown off them as long spikes ("lines shooting from the NPCs"), on some
frames and not others. CPU skinning (`--cpu-skinning`) drew the same frames
clean, and so does this, with GPU skinning on (scratch run 2026-10-19: the town
horse spiked in 2 of 3 frames before, 0 of 11 after). Desktop GL draws the same
models with it. A macro per WEIGHT_COUNT, same arithmetic and order as the
function. Not __SWITCH__-guarded: a GLSL library file every GLES target reads.

diff --git a/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_DualQuaternion.glsl b/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_DualQuaternion.glsl
index e9a64a3f2..3efbb91c3 100644
--- a/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_DualQuaternion.glsl
+++ b/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_DualQuaternion.glsl
@@ -157,16 +157,16 @@ void blendBonesDQ(mat2x4 bones_dq[BONE_COUNT], vec4 indices, vec4 weights, out m
 	blendDQ /= length(blendDQ[0]); // normalise dual quaternion
 }
 
-void blendBonesMat3x4(mat3x4 bones_mat[BONE_COUNT], vec4 indices, vec4 weights, out mat3x4 blendMat)
-{
-	blendMat = bones_mat[int(indices.x)] * weights.x;
-#if WEIGHT_COUNT > 1
-	blendMat += bones_mat[int(indices.y)] * weights.y;
-#endif
-#if WEIGHT_COUNT > 2
-	blendMat += bones_mat[int(indices.z)] * weights.z;
-#endif
+// Torchlight: a macro, not a function. As a function the whole bone array is
+// an `in` parameter - a copy of up to BONE_COUNT matrices per vertex - and
+// Adreno's compiler gets it wrong: vertices thrown off the model as spikes.
+// Indexing the uniform where it is declared draws the same blend.
 #if WEIGHT_COUNT > 3
-	blendMat += bones_mat[int(indices.w)] * weights.w;
-#endif
-}
\ No newline at end of file
+#define blendBonesMat3x4(bones_mat, indices, weights, blendMat) blendMat = bones_mat[int(indices.x)] * weights.x + bones_mat[int(indices.y)] * weights.y + bones_mat[int(indices.z)] * weights.z + bones_mat[int(indices.w)] * weights.w
+#elif WEIGHT_COUNT > 2
+#define blendBonesMat3x4(bones_mat, indices, weights, blendMat) blendMat = bones_mat[int(indices.x)] * weights.x + bones_mat[int(indices.y)] * weights.y + bones_mat[int(indices.z)] * weights.z
+#elif WEIGHT_COUNT > 1
+#define blendBonesMat3x4(bones_mat, indices, weights, blendMat) blendMat = bones_mat[int(indices.x)] * weights.x + bones_mat[int(indices.y)] * weights.y
+#else
+#define blendBonesMat3x4(bones_mat, indices, weights, blendMat) blendMat = bones_mat[int(indices.x)] * weights.x
+#endif
\ No newline at end of file
